00:01:16,540 --> 00:01:18,180
 to transfer function is only valid

36
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:20,540
 when those models are themselves linear.

37
00:01:20,540 --> 00:01:21,740
 Secondly, you're probably thinking,

38
00:01:21,740 --> 00:01:23,380
 where is this equation coming from?

39
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:25,620
 You've just thrown this kind of matrix equation

40
00:01:25,620 --> 00:01:26,820
 that involves an inverse at me,

41
00:01:26,820 --> 00:01:28,660
 and I have no idea where it's came from.

42
00:01:28,660 --> 00:01:30,460
 That's a really good point.

43
00:01:30,460 --> 00:01:32,620
 derivations are not something I like to normally show

44
00:01:32,620 --> 00:01:35,820
 in class, I think that puts most people to sleep.

45
00:01:35,820 --> 00:01:37,780
 But actually, the derivation of this equation

46
00:01:37,780 --> 00:01:39,220
 of how to go for a state-space model

47
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:42,020
 to transfer function, I think is super interesting.

48
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:43,220
 So let me switch to the iPad

49
00:01:43,220 --> 00:01:44,980
 and we'll work through that together now.

50
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:46,780
 All right, I've pulled up the kind of matrix form

51
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:48,220
 of a state-space equation here.

52
00:01:48,220 --> 00:01:50,500
 Z dot is a z plus b u,

53
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:53,380
 and y is c z plus d u.

54
00:01:53,380 --> 00:01:55,460
 Keep in mind that when we've written it this way,

55
00:01:55,460 --> 00:01:57,460
 we're talking about functions of time, right?

56
00:01:57,460 --> 00:01:59,500
 Z dot the vector is a function of time,

57
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:01,620
 y the vector is a function of time,

58
00:02:01,620 --> 00:02:04,900
 as is kind of z the vector and u the vector, right?

59
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:08,820
 This is z of t, and this is u of t.

60
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:10,420
 But our transfer function isn't defined

61
00:02:10,420 --> 00:02:12,020
 in terms of functions of time, right?

62
00:02:12,020 --> 00:02:13,300
 It's a Laplace space.

63
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:16,660
 It's g of s, the transfer function is y of s,

64
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:18,780
 the output of Laplace space on u of s,

65
00:02:18,780 --> 00:02:20,980
 the input of Laplace space.

66
00:02:20,980 --> 00:02:23,540
 So I guess the first step in kind of any derivation

67
00:02:23,540 --> 00:02:25,860
 of the link, a state-space model to a transfer function

68
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:28,220
 should probably involve a Laplace transform.

69
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:31,420
 So let's Laplace transform both of these equations.

70
00:02:31,420 --> 00:02:33,180
 So if I Laplace transform the first,

71
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:36,540
 I'm going to have Laplace transform of z dot

72
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:40,700
 is Laplace transform of a z plus b u,

73
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:44,660
 and I'm going to have that Laplace transform of y

74
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:48,060
 is Laplace transform of c z plus d u.

75
00:02:49,380 --> 00:02:51,060
 Okay, cool.

76
00:02:51,060 --> 00:02:53,300
 If I keep going, I'm going to do two at a time.

77
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:54,820
 So bear with me.

78
00:02:54,820 --> 00:02:56,740
 The Laplace transform of z dot,

79
00:02:56,740 --> 00:02:59,740
 well, z dot is a vector full of time varying functions.

80
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:01,660
 And so I can kind of apply the same rule

81
00:03:01,660 --> 00:03:03,180
 that I would to each of those functions

82
00:03:03,180 --> 00:03:05,060
 to a vector of those functions, right?

83
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:07,300
 The Laplace transform of the derivative of a thing

84
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:11,020
 is in general s times the Laplace transform of that thing, right?

85
00:03:11,020 --> 00:03:12,900
 So the Laplace transform of z dot,

86
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:15,260
 well, that's just going to be s times

87
00:03:15,260 --> 00:03:17,940
 kind of a big z of s, right?

88
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:20,700
 It's like if I took the Laplace transform of z,

89
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:22,380
 which would be z of s,

90
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:25,100
 but I multiply it by s to account for that derivative.

91
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:26,580
 Okay, cool.

92
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:27,620
 Then I'm going to,

93
00:03:27,620 --> 00:03:29,620
 well, the Laplace transform is a linear operator,

94
00:03:29,620 --> 00:03:32,060
 so I can apply it to each of these things individually.

95
00:03:32,060 --> 00:03:34,060
 And so I'm going to kind of have

96
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:37,460
 Laplace transform of a z will become a big z of s.

97
00:03:38,460 --> 00:03:40,220
 And Laplace transform of b u

98
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:44,820
 will that's going to be kind of b multiplied by b u of s.

99
00:03:45,740 --> 00:03:47,860
 And for the second equation, Laplace transform of y

100
00:03:47,860 --> 00:03:51,420
 is going to be y of s equals Laplace transform of c z.

101
00:03:51,420 --> 00:03:55,260
 So c big z of s plus Laplace transform of d u

102
00:03:55,260 --> 00:03:57,180
 is going to be d big u of s.

103
00:03:58,180 --> 00:04:00,460
 Okay, let's see.

104
00:04:00,460 --> 00:04:02,580
 So this is probably the equation I want to be working with

105
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:05,060
 because it kind of relates to y to u,

106
00:04:05,060 --> 00:04:08,500
 but it has this pesky kind of z of s in there.

107
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:09,340
 Though I postulate,

108
00:04:09,340 --> 00:04:10,620
 I might be able to get rid of that

109
00:04:10,620 --> 00:04:13,340
 from kind of the state equation that's above.

110
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:15,620
 So let me rearrange that state equation slightly.

111
00:04:15,620 --> 00:04:20,740
 I'll have s z of s minus a z of s

112
00:04:20,740 --> 00:04:23,220
 is equal to b u of s.

113
00:04:23,220 --> 00:04:24,660
 That looks a bit more promising.

114
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:25,980
 I can factor the z of s.

115
00:04:25,980 --> 00:04:30,420
 And I'm going to have z of s outside of s minus a.

116
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:33,620
 And that's going to be b u.

117
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:36,260
 And I really should be kind of sensible here

118
00:04:36,260 --> 00:04:39,260
 that s is a scalar and a is a matrix.

119
00:04:39,260 --> 00:04:41,260
 So if I'd like to perform this kind of subtraction,

120
00:04:41,260 --> 00:04:43,500
 if I want to actually factor these things out,

121
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:46,660
 then really, there should be written as s i

122
00:04:46,660 --> 00:04:48,500
 and identity matrix, right?

123
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:50,380
 If I want to actually factor these things

124
00:04:50,380 --> 00:04:52,940
 out as if they were functions and not matrices,

125
00:04:52,940 --> 00:04:56,020
 I need to include that identity matrix in there.

126
00:04:56,020 --> 00:04:57,820
 Okay, cool.

127
00:04:57,820 --> 00:04:59,260
 And if you do some rearranging,

128
00:04:59,260 --> 00:05:07,260
 then this is z of s is kind of s i minus a inverse times b u

129
00:05:07,260 --> 00:05:08,660
 of s, right?

130
00:05:08,660 --> 00:05:11,300
 Because you have that kind of inverse operation

131
00:05:11,300 --> 00:05:13,300
 is like dividing for matrices, right?

132
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:16,420
 To divide by s i minus a or both sides,

133
00:05:16,420 --> 00:05:19,220
 I need to kind of multiply by the matrix inverse.

134
00:05:19,260 --> 00:05:21,220
 And I'm going to do it on the left.

135
00:05:21,220 --> 00:05:24,140
 Okay, well, I can substitute this into my equation

136
00:05:24,140 --> 00:05:25,340
 for y now, right?

137
00:05:25,340 --> 00:05:29,020
 So y was previously c times z of s.

138
00:05:29,020 --> 00:05:34,020
 So I'm going to end up with c s i minus a inverse b u of s.

139
00:05:38,380 --> 00:05:41,540
 And I'm going to end up with plus d u of s.

140
00:05:45,220 --> 00:05:46,540
 Okay, cool.

141
00:05:46,540 --> 00:05:49,260
 Let's see, factoring out the u of s,

142
00:05:49,260 --> 00:05:54,260
 I'm going to have c s i minus a inverse b plus d

143
00:05:56,780 --> 00:06:00,140
 amounts of u of s.

144
00:06:00,140 --> 00:06:02,700
 And hey, this is kind of the formula I promised you

145
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:04,860
 that you'd get for g of s, right?

146
00:06:04,860 --> 00:06:09,380
 c s i minus a inverse plus d or multiply by b plus d.

147
00:06:10,340 --> 00:06:13,140
 So a pretty satisfying derivation, if you ask me,

148
00:06:13,140 --> 00:06:15,660
 the kind of tricky bits being here

149
00:06:15,740 --> 00:06:18,180
 we need to work out that we include an identity matrix

150
00:06:18,180 --> 00:06:20,740
 to bring something out the front and kind of here

151
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:22,180
 where if we want to divide something,

152
00:06:22,180 --> 00:06:24,700
 we need to kind of do a matrix inverse multiply

153
00:06:24,700 --> 00:06:25,860
 by instead of a divide.

154
00:06:25,860 --> 00:06:28,620
 But if you kind of up with your matrix multiplication,

155
00:06:28,620 --> 00:06:30,740
 if you can think back to math 1120,

156
00:06:30,740 --> 00:06:32,820
 these shouldn't be obscure concepts to you

157
00:06:32,820 --> 00:06:35,660
 though I do concede that was last year.

158
00:06:35,660 --> 00:06:37,980
 And so I understand if it's not entirely fresh

159
00:06:37,980 --> 00:06:39,340
 in your brain.

160
00:06:39,340 --> 00:06:41,180
 Anyway, a fun little derivation,

161
00:06:41,180 --> 00:06:43,100
 probably not one you need to memorize,

162
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:45,500
 but it's good to know where these equations come from.

163
00:06:45,780 --> 00:06:47,580
 In the future, you can just apply the equation

164
00:06:47,580 --> 00:06:49,340
 or more likely just kind of get MATLAB

